WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
Mark Zuckerberg wants to build the metaverse,&nbsp;
but the crypto community has a different plan.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:09.280
Now, they might not agree on exactly which crypto&nbsp;
project is best. But one thing is for sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.040
they don’t think Zuckerberg stands&nbsp;
a chance against decentralization.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.360
These crypto folks are sick and tired of&nbsp;
having one person exercise absolute control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.920
and they’re funding independent projects&nbsp;
left and right that aim to unseat Zuckerberg.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:26.560
There’s a problem though. Even though trillions&nbsp;
of dollars have poured into cryptocurrencies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.760
Zuck and the other big tech companies&nbsp;
aren’t going down without a fight.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.080
Facebook, Apple, Microsoft, and Google are used&nbsp;
by billions of people, and they aren’t just going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.800
to turn over the keys to kingdom to a bunch of&nbsp;
anonymous hackers with pixelated profile pictures.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.120
It’s a knockdown, drag-out fight, and in&nbsp;
order to understand who’s likely to prevail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.480
we need to investigate 3 important&nbsp;
technological developments.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.720
Crypto will clearly play some&nbsp;
sort of role in the metaverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.120
so let’s dig into the battle for payments&nbsp;
between Mark Zuckerberg and the crypto community.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.080
First up, let’s talk about what Facebook has&nbsp;
done in crypto so far. It’s easy to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.240
off Zuckerberg as a player in the crypto&nbsp;
world, but he’s extremely well connected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.000
After all, one of his longest standing&nbsp;
board members is Marc Andreessen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.840
who’s venture capital firm runs a&nbsp;
multi-billion dollar crypto fund.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.960
That fund has invested in crypto exchanges&nbsp;
like CoinBase, NFT marketplaces like OpenSea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.800
and even new blockchains like Solana.&nbsp;
So Zuckerberg is clearly well aware of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.880
the growing importance of crypto, but a&nbsp;
few years ago he ran into a big problem.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.800
See, it was becoming abundantly clear that&nbsp;
crypto was going to be an important technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.600
going forward, but Facebook didn’t really&nbsp;
have a way to tap into that world. Billions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.440
of people used Facebook, WhatsApp, and Instagram&nbsp;
to communicate with each other, but when it came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:40.400
to sending each other money, they usually had to&nbsp;
go use another service, like PayPal or Cash App.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.080
So, Zuckerberg saw an opportunity&nbsp;
to change this. The idea was simple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.840
He would launch a new cryptocurrency and&nbsp;
then integrate it directly into Facebook.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.840
This new project would be called Libra,&nbsp;
which isn’t quite as catchy as ZuckBucks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.440
but there was a lot at stake, so I get why&nbsp;
they wanted to use a more professional name.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.280
The first question that came up immediately&nbsp;
was: “why not just use bitcoin?”

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.720
Tens of millions of people were already&nbsp;
using Bitcoin, but the network was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.320
too slow for Zuckerberg’s vision.&nbsp;
Bitcoin could only handle around 7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.720
transactions per second, but Libra would&nbsp;
scale to thousands of transactions per second.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:15.120
Sounds good in theory, but when you’re dealing&nbsp;
with crypto, everything is about trade-offs.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.840
Bitcoin has attracted so many devotees&nbsp;
precisely because there is no need to trust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.280
anyone, you can verify the entire&nbsp;
blockchain yourself if you like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.320
But this comes at the cost of&nbsp;
efficiency of transactions.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.640
At the opposite end of this spectrum would&nbsp;
be a coin completely controlled by Facebook.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:34.400
You’d have to trust them completely, but&nbsp;
they could just store all transactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.680
in a centralized database and&nbsp;
maximize transaction speed.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.760
Now, Zuckerberg and his team realized early&nbsp;
on that this would be a non-starter. People&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.040
were having a hard enough time trusting&nbsp;
Facebook with pictures of their cats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.320
so giving Zuckerberg complete control&nbsp;
over their money would be a stretch.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:54.080
So the Libra team came up with a compromise. They&nbsp;
would build something that sat halfway between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.720
the fully decentralized Bitcoin blockchain&nbsp;
and a centralized database like Visa. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.920
distributed ledger structure would allow Libra to&nbsp;
be the best of both worlds, at least in theory.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.640
Instead of Facebook having&nbsp;
total control over the network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.560
28 separate organizations would work&nbsp;
together to validate transactions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.280
These validators were all big companies,&nbsp;
like PayPal and Mastercard, and they each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:20.160
put up $10 million dollars and promised to&nbsp;
provide computing power on an ongoing basis.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:23.760
This had a big drawback though. Instead&nbsp;
of allowing users to see the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.240
history of transactions, Libra would only&nbsp;
expose the current state of the ledger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.080
This would make it faster, but you’d&nbsp;
have to trust these big companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.920
and the crypto community had plenty of&nbsp;
other options. Fans of Bitcoin weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.520
likely to adopt Libra anytime soon, as&nbsp;
long as big companies were involved.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.280
That wasn’t even the biggest issue though.&nbsp;
Plenty of people have no problem trusting large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.520
corporations with their money and data. In fact,&nbsp;
most people don’t even think about it. So telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.280
people that Libra is semi-decentralized&nbsp;
isn’t that big of a selling point.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:56.240
See, anytime a new technology comes along, there&nbsp;
are switching costs. People naturally develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:01.120
habits and getting them to ditch what they’re&nbsp;
used to in favor of something new is never easy.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.760
When credit cards first came along, they were&nbsp;
basically just competing with cash and checks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.480
Switching to a credit card meant&nbsp;
that if someone stole your wallet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.440
you could quickly disable the card and return any&nbsp;
lost funds. That was a huge upgrade from cash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.440
so people switched over in droves.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.840
But now, credit cards are good enough for most&nbsp;
people. In order for Libra to become massively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.720
successful, it couldn’t just be 10% better&nbsp;
than credit cards, it needed to be 100% better.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.120
So Zuckerberg and the Libra team devised a plan&nbsp;
of attack. Being faster than bitcoin was a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:34.560
start, but it wasn’t enough. Volatility had always&nbsp;
been a natural part of all cryptocurrencies, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:38.320
while most day traders loved this&nbsp;
feature, for any average consumer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.000
it would cause major problems. What if&nbsp;
you go to buy your groceries with Libra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.120
and the value collapses while you’re&nbsp;
standing in the checkout line?

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.840
The solution was backing Libra with a basket of&nbsp;
more traditional, and more stable currencies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:53.520
The price still might fluctuate, but it&nbsp;
should be a lot more stable than bitcoin.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.160
This created a new problem though, and this would&nbsp;
ultimately lead to the early demise of Libra.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.520
See, the United States government was&nbsp;
already starting to take a hard look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.560
at the platform that Zuckerberg had&nbsp;
built over the last decade. More and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:08.640
more regulators were arguing that Facebook&nbsp;
had too much control over social media.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.880
If the Libra project worked as planned, that&nbsp;
control would now expand to the financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.120
sector and could potentially undermine the&nbsp;
monetary sovereignty of the United States.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:21.120
Zuckerberg knew that he had to tread&nbsp;
lightly here. The spotlight was on him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.280
and ruffling feathers in Washington could&nbsp;
have disastrous consequences. He announced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:29.040
that he would not launch the currency until&nbsp;
he had full approval from the government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.880
but the challenge was too much to&nbsp;
overcome, so they shut it down.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.440
Now, while all this craziness was&nbsp;
happening with the Libra project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.840
the rest of the crypto world was absolutely&nbsp;
exploding. Billions of dollars flowed into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:46.560
crypto projects and the top developers started&nbsp;
laying some really powerful foundations.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.360
The term “metaverse” wasn’t&nbsp;
even in widespread use yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:53.280
and most crypto devs probably weren’t&nbsp;
even thinking about Mark Zuckerberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:58.320
but these new blockchain technologies would wind&nbsp;
up growing into Zuck’s biggest challenge yet.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.560
It’s important to recognize that the term&nbsp;
“metaverse” doesn’t just refer to a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:06.640
game or VR experience. We’re talking about&nbsp;
the next phase of the internet yet. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.600
like mobile phones wound up evolving everyday&nbsp;
activities and creating entire opportunities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:15.920
the metaverse is basically just a catchall&nbsp;
term for the next phase of the internet.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:19.920
In fact, the term “metaverse” has&nbsp;
been so completely co-opted by Zuck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.200
that a large portion of the crypto&nbsp;
community has moved to calling this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:27.360
whole idea “Web3”. But regardless&nbsp;
of what term we use to describe it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.120
clearly payments are going to play an&nbsp;
important role in the future of technology.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:34.880
This can be hard to see sometimes though.&nbsp;
Especially when cartoons are selling for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:39.040
millions of dollars and Elon is tweeting&nbsp;
about dog money. There is a ton of noise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:42.880
in the crypto space, as well plenty&nbsp;
of scams, so be careful out there.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:46.800
And quickly, I’ll never ask you to message&nbsp;
a random phone number in the comments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:48.400
but you can subscribe to the channel.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:54.240
Now, once we separate out all the junk and get&nbsp;
to the core of this technology, how can crypto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:58.800
actually help build the metaverse? Well, the&nbsp;
first area is interoperability. Right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.920
that basically just means bringing a digital asset&nbsp;
from one game to another. Which might seem silly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:08.960
but remember, the virtual economy already&nbsp;
generates over $50 billion dollars per year.

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:14.480
But honestly, who cares if you can bring&nbsp;
a Minecraft skin into Call of Duty? Does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:19.280
that really create any meaningful value or&nbsp;
advance technology even the slightest bit?

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.320
As silly as this all sounds, I actually do think&nbsp;
it’s an important step forward. Here’s why.

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:28.720
Right now, every traditional tech company&nbsp;
is building its own little walled garden.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:33.360
Epic Games makes Fortnite. You buy a skin&nbsp;
in Fortnite, it lives in Fortnite forever.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:37.040
Now Epic Games could allow digital&nbsp;
assets from other games to transfer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:41.280
into Fortnite. They might need to build some&nbsp;
complex pipelines to port the assets over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:46.080
or define an open standard, but they could do&nbsp;
it. The only reason they don’t is that it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:50.720
hurt their business. Epic wants to be the only&nbsp;
company that can sell dancing bananas in Fortnite.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:55.040
But, if crypto can break down these walled&nbsp;
gardens, the playing field will get a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:58.560
more competitive. Creators building&nbsp;
any small piece of the metaverse will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.080
be able to ensure that what they build&nbsp;
continues to make them money forever.

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:06.560
If you can accept the fact that the $50 billion&nbsp;
dollar virtual economy is basically “real” at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:11.600
point, it becomes a lot clearer why crypto might&nbsp;
prove useful here. A virtual asset might be bought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:16.080
in one game, and then sold in another. Making&nbsp;
sure the creator gets a small royalty payment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:21.280
would be incredibly complex without crypto,&nbsp;
but NFTs make this both simple and reliable.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:24.800
This allows developers and creators to&nbsp;
stop worrying about building an entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:29.680
new walled garden, but instead focus on&nbsp;
making useful building blocks. One person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:34.720
builds some virtual land, then another develops&nbsp;
virtual houses, and yes, then someone probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.560
makes a dancing banana. Actually, the dancing&nbsp;
banana probably comes first. Let’s be honest.

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:41.680
Now, to be clear, we are clearly&nbsp;
still years from all of this coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:45.360
together into anything resembling the&nbsp;
Metaverse that Zuck is talking about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.640
Most of these projects are still in&nbsp;
their infancy and only a few have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:52.480
built significant user bases, but the&nbsp;
building blocks are starting to form.

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:55.840
The immense wealth created by the&nbsp;
crypto boom over the past decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:00.160
really can’t be discounted. Bitcoin alone&nbsp;
has minted dozens of new billionaires,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:04.400
and most of those folks aren’t about to put&nbsp;
their newfound wealth in traditional tech stocks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:08.320
They are investing in the next generation&nbsp;
of crypto projects and helping build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:11.280
infrastructure for what they see&nbsp;
as the future of the internet.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:15.680
Obviously Zuckerberg isn’t the only person the&nbsp;
crypto crowd is up against though. Tons of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:21.280
passionately hate crypto and want NFTs to fail,&nbsp;
but most of their criticisms are pretty flawed.

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:26.080
You’ll commonly hear people say that NFTs are&nbsp;
bad because of their environmental impact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:31.360
but there are already alternative blockchains&nbsp;
that use less energy per transaction than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:35.520
a single Google search. You’ll also&nbsp;
hear people point out that NFTs are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:40.400
just links to files stored on centralized&nbsp;
servers, and that’s true for many projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:46.480
but not all. Some projects do in fact store all&nbsp;
of the data related to the NFT on the blockchain.

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:51.600
But technical arguments aside, by far the biggest&nbsp;
critique you’ll hear is around prices. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:56.800
anti-crypto crowd will jump at the opportunity to&nbsp;
point out that we’re in a bubble and prices can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:00.800
possibly stay this high. Just look at what&nbsp;
happened during the dot com boom and bust.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:04.480
And that’s a good point, we should look&nbsp;
at what happened during the dot com boom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:09.040
because I actually think history could tell us&nbsp;
a lot about what the future will look like here.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:12.880
No one denies that the year 2000 was&nbsp;
a crazy time for technology investing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:14.480
Companies were going public&nbsp;
burning millions of dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:19.680
all while providing very little real value&nbsp;
to everyday people. There was a vague idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:24.560
that this technology might be useful one day, but&nbsp;
few of the business models made any real sense.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:29.200
Then, the market crashed. People lost stock&nbsp;
portfolios, lots of people lost their jobs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:33.920
But there was a silver lining to all this&nbsp;
market mayhem. During the dot com bubble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:39.040
investors had financed huge infrastructure&nbsp;
projects focused on laying fiber optic cable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:44.160
in the ground. Actually connecting every computer&nbsp;
to the internet was an incredibly costly endeavor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:47.360
but without these connections,&nbsp;
there would be no internet industry.

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:52.000
So the investors ponied up millions of dollars&nbsp;
and got the fiber into the ground. After the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.080
dot com crash, it looked like a waste. All&nbsp;
those high-flying tech companies that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:00.560
planning on using this new fiber were now&nbsp;
bankrupt, so were these cables now useless?

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:05.040
Well, not quite. With the infrastructure now&nbsp;
available, people started building again, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:12.800
pretty quickly. On October 9th, 2002, the NASDAQ&nbsp;
hit a new low, down 78% from its peak. Just over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:17.760
one year later, a young Mark Zuckerberg would hit&nbsp;
publish on the first version of thefacebook.com.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:20.000
So how does this relate to crypto? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.320
even if we are in a bubble, it’s possible&nbsp;
that the foundational technologies that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:28.640
being built today will prove incredibly&nbsp;
useful to a future batch of companies.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:32.480
And it can be useful to go back even&nbsp;
further in time, to the turn of the century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:36.800
The pace of technological progress was really&nbsp;
staggering during the industrial revolution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:42.000
Engineers had partnered with financers like never&nbsp;
before and were taking on increasingly ambitious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.800
projects. Westinghouse and Edison were duking it&nbsp;
out to build the best system for electrification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:51.360
Henry Ford was building the first automobile. And&nbsp;
the Wright Brothers were inventing the airplane.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:53.680
The telegraph industry created a boom of capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:57.360
and engineering talent in search of the&nbsp;
next big thing. Lots of people failed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:00.880
but some people landed on some&nbsp;
absolutely groundbreaking territory.

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:04.880
So most crypto supporters are hoping&nbsp;
for something similar to unfold here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:09.760
The money is clearly already there, and lots of&nbsp;
smart engineers have moved over to building crypto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:14.000
projects. That puts Zuckerberg in a tricky&nbsp;
position. If one of these projects breaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:18.080
out and becomes essential to the metaverse,&nbsp;
he could be stuck fighting on his back foot.

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:20.720
But in order to understand&nbsp;
exactly how this might play out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:24.960
we need to dive into the history of one of&nbsp;
the fastest growing blockchains in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:32.960
Tons of metaverse projects are already being built&nbsp;
on top of it, so watch this video to find out why.

